  <section id="model-data" class="pck-section" aria-labelledby="data-title">
    <div class="pck-page pck-split pck-split-data">
      <div class="pck-split-text">
        <p class="pck-kicker">From <a href="https://providers.dev">providers.dev</a></p>
        <h2 id="data-title">Model data</h2>
        <p>
          Pi's model catalog doesn't say how long most providers keep a prompt cache, and for some models its input window is smaller than what the provider accepts. Without a
          lifetime there is no countdown, no compacting before expiry and no warming; with a window that is too small, Pi compacts long before it has to.
        </p>
        <p>
          providers.dev publishes both, with prices, for each model at each provider and access method (API key or subscription login), from the providers' documentation and
          from evaluations. The extension puts them into the <code>modelOverrides</code> of <code>~/.pi/agent/models.json</code>, the place Pi has for it, so Pi's own warming
          and compaction use the same numbers as the countdown.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="pck-facts">
        <li><strong>What is written.</strong> providers.dev's input window replaces Pi's (one known only from an evaluation can raise Pi's, never lower it). Cache lifetimes only for a tier Pi has no lifetime for; prices and the output limit only where Pi has none.</li>
        <li><strong>Your own values stay.</strong> Values you set in <code>models.json</code> yourself are never touched.</li>
        <li><strong>What is sent.</strong> The names of the providers Pi has credentials for, as query parameters to a public read-only API. No key, no account, nothing about your sessions or prompts.</li>
        <li><strong>When.</strong> At the start of an interactive session, when the saved data is a day old. Nothing is fetched on its own in print or JSON mode or with <code>PI_OFFLINE</code> set.</li>
        <li><strong>Off.</strong> <code>"modelData": false</code> takes the values it wrote out of <code>models.json</code>, which brings Pi's own back.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="install" class="pck-section" aria-labelledby="install-title">
    <div class="pck-page">
      <header class="pck-head">
        <h2 id="install-title">Install</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="pck-install">
        <div class="pck-install-main">
          <div class="pck-code">
            <pre><code>{install}</code></pre>
            <button type="button" class="copy" data-copy={install} aria-label="Copy the install command">Copy</button>
          </div>
          <p>
            Then run <code>/reload</code>: Pi loads extensions only at startup or on <code>/reload</code>. Compacting from the cache, compacting when full, compacting before
            expiry and model data from providers.dev are on by default; keeping the cache warm is off until you set <code>keepWarmMinutes</code>. Update it
            with <code>pi update --extensions</code>.
          </p>
          <p class="pck-links">
            <a class="button" href={npm}>pi-cache-keeper on npm</a>
            <a class="button" href={github}>Source on GitHub</a>
            <a class="button" href={`${github}/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md`}>Changelog</a>
          </p>
        </div>
        <section class="pck-card" aria-labelledby="needs-title">
          <h3 id="needs-title">Requirements</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Pi 1.6.0 or newer.</strong></li>
            <li>No runtime dependencies and no install scripts.</li>
            <li>Published to npm by GitHub Actions, with provenance.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
      </div>

      <h3 class="pck-sub-title pck-sub-title-gap" id="providers">Providers</h3>
      <div class="pck-table-wrap">
